  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold airmass builds into the region this \nweekend. Freeze Warnings are in effect for tonight. Another \nround of frost is possible Sunday night into Monday morning.\n\nA cold front continues to work through southeast New Jersey and\nDelmarva this afternoon as low pressure moves away. Any \nlingering light rain showers will taper off as the afternoon \nprogresses. A tight pressure gradient exists between the \ndeparting low and high pressure building into the Central and \nSouthern Plains. North winds 15 to 20 mph with 25 to 35 mph \ngusts going into this afternoon should gradually dwindle toward\nsunset.\n\nTonight, temperatures drop into the 30s nearly throughout, with\nlows down to or below freezing for the southern Poconos, Lehigh\nValley, and northwest New Jersey. Maintained freeze warnings as\ninherited, but mixing from lingering wind may preclude the \nsoutheastern extent of them from verifying. Confidence not high \nenough to cancel, however.\n\nHigh temperatures on Sunday will then only be in the 50s for \nmost of the region despite mostly sunny skies. While another \nnight of temperatures below freezing is not expected, conditions\nwill once again potentially favor the development of some \nfrost, mainly confined to the Lehigh Valley, Poconos and \nnorthern New Jersey. Lows are expected to be several degrees \nwarmer than Saturday night mainly in the mid to upper 30s. \nAdditional headlines can't be ruled out for Sunday night if \nfrost potential is realized where the growing season remains \nactive, though the growing season may be ended in these areas by\nthe freeze tonight.\n\nTemperatures will then begin to slowly moderate Sunday night \ninto early next week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Uncertainty persists regarding a nor'easter \ndeveloping off the coast of the southern Mid-Atlantic that may \ncause unsettled conditions and hazardous marine impacts early \nnext week.\n\nThe base of a deep trough axis will pass through the region later \nSaturday. As it does so, the trough axis will then cutoff into an \nupper level low which will meander over the southern Mid-Atlantic \ncoast into early next week. At the same time, strong and expansive \nhigh pressure will be building over the Ohio Valley. While \nconfidence is increasing regarding a nor'easter developing off the \nNorth Carolina coast later this weekend, it must be said there \nremains a vast amount of uncertainty with regards to potential \nimpacts to our region into next week. \n\nConsiderable differences amongst global/ensemble guidance exists \nwith regards to just how close/long this system will get/last in our \nregion. Relative to the previous forecast discussion, there has not \nbeen much change. The EC ensemble member spread is fairly tightly \nclustered through the early part of next week, and then begins to \ndiverge, but generally has the low tracking northward and then \nmeandering off the Mid-Atlantic coast into mid or even late week. \nThe GEFS members are similar until about mid-week when they begin to \ndiverge more substantially. All of this being said, it doesn't \nappear likely that there will be significant impacts inland from \nthis system, even though they can't be entirely ruled out at this \ntime. What is increasingly likely though is at least some impacts to \ncoastal communities with periods of gusty northeast winds, some \nshowers, and potentially some coastal flooding concerns, potentially \nfor multiple days next week. We will continue to monitor the \nprogress of this developing system.",
